<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{{NAME}}</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="./src/style.css" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <!--
      The markup below is the accessibility baseline, not decoration. The webview
      builds the OS accessibility tree from these elements, so a page is only as
      navigable as its roles, labels and heading order:

        - `<main>` is the one landmark a reader can jump to.
        - Every form control has a `<label>`; `for`/`id` is what binds it, and an
          input with a placeholder and no label is announced as "edit text".
        - `<h1>` then `<h2>`, never a skipped level: the heading list is the
          reader's table of contents.
        - `aria-live="polite"` on the result region is what makes a command's
          answer audible without the user hunting for it.
        - Buttons are `<button type="...">`, so Enter and Space both activate them
          and the role is not a guess from a click handler.
    -->
    <main>
      <h1>{{NAME}}</h1>
      <p class="hint">A native command round-trip: TypeScript calls Rust, Rust returns data.</p>
      <form id="greet">
        <label for="name">Name</label>
        <input id="name" value="world" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" />
        <button type="submit">Invoke</button>
      </form>
      <p id="result" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">waiting</p>
      <div class="row">
        <button id="slow" type="button" class="ghost">Slow async task</button>
        <button id="settings" type="button" class="ghost">Open settings window</button>
      </div>
    </main>
    <script type="module" src="./src/main.ts"></script>
  </body>
</html>
